## 1. What the service is

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-IDENTITY] An HTTP service on the bus master, at port 8420

- what: an HTTP service running on the bus master, storing configs, assets, the namespace registry and workload definitions. All endpoints return JSON unless otherwise noted.
- base-url: `http://{bus_master}:8420/api/v1`.
- port-history: §7 records that the port is 8420 from `COMPOSEIO_PORT`, and that the `5100` the section specified until 2026-08-14 was never the port the service listens on. The correction is carried across with its history because a reader may still hold the old number.
- transport-role: §6.4 (`BP-TRANSPORT-HTTP`) places this service outside the real-time path — "HTTP is not a real-time message transport." Real-time messages flow over MQTT or CAN; HTTP is for config, assets, state queries and system management.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §6.4 and §7 preamble
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-ROLE] Source of truth for the fleet, so a replacement module self-configures

- purpose: DP-CIO-23 states the bus provides a persistent storage service holding compiled configs, large assets (images, audio, firmware) and system-wide state (namespace registry, device roster). Modules pull their configuration from it on boot rather than requiring individual manual pushes.
- config-lifecycle-doctrine: builder edits YAML on their laptop, `composeio push` writes the compiled binary to bus storage under the target namespace, and any device configured for that namespace picks it up — immediately if online, or on next boot. No per-device flashing.
- replacement-story: replacing a dead module is "power on, device announces its role, bus storage delivers its config, device self-configures." DP-CIO-23 states the failure test directly: if replacing a dead module requires locating the original config file and manually re-pushing it, the design has failed.
- large-assets: anything too large for a bus message (DP-CIO-18) lives here and is referenced by `bus://` URI, e.g. `{namespace: "now_playing", art_url: "bus://assets/album_art/current.jpg"}`.
- registry-persistence: DP-CIO-23 claims the store maintains a registry of all known namespaces, their state engines, current values, and which devices publish and subscribe — enabling auto-discovery, introspection and a `composeio status` view of the whole system. ⚠ §7.2 CONTRADICTS the richer half of this claim; see [CXC-BUSSTORE-NOT-OBSERVER] and [CXC-BUSSTORE-SOURCES-DISAGREE].
- backup-property: the entire system's configuration, assets and namespace registry live in one location. Back it up, restore it to a new bus master, power on the devices, and the system self-reconstructs — called out as critical for deployable installations such as vehicle head units, escape rooms and permanent props.
- source-section: spec/00-composeio-design-principles.smd DP-CIO-23
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-CACHE-DOCTRINE] Local flash is a cache, not the source of truth

- rule: DP-CIO-23 states it as a headline — devices cache their last-pulled config in local flash for resilience (DP-CIO-11). If bus storage is unavailable on boot, the device runs from its cached config. When the store becomes available again, the device checks for updates and pulls if the config has changed, comparing version or checksum.
- named-pattern: the source calls this the Docker registry pattern — pull when available, run local when not.
- staleness-warning-in-source: §8.3's cross-reference adds that booting from cache while re-requesting is resilient but can render a months-old scene and look perfectly healthy, and states that cache MUST be paired with a visible staleness indication.
- where-implemented: EMPTY. The sources state the doctrine and do not name the module, key or storage medium that implements it. Not filled in here from code.
- source-section: spec/00-composeio-design-principles.smd DP-CIO-23; spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §8.3 cross-reference
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-NOT-OBSERVER] The store is PASSIVE — no write has a side effect, and it cannot see the bus

- rule: §7 states this as a correction to earlier prose. Bus storage holds no bus handle. It does not observe traffic, publish, or notify.
- consequence-config: a `PUT /api/v1/config/{module_id}` writes the file and returns. The previously-documented "publishes `config_push` on PUT" DOES NOT HAPPEN. A module is NOT notified of a new config by this call; it learns on its next boot, or by the bus-config path, which §7 stresses is a different mechanism on a different transport. Any flow assuming an HTTP PUT reaches a live module is wrong.
- consequence-workload: `PUT /api/v1/workloads/{id}` writes the JSON and returns; it does not notify or start anything. A service hub running the workload must be polling or otherwise triggered.
- consequence-registry: the namespace registry is a passive store — it persists whatever a client PUTs and returns it verbatim. It does not derive publishers, subscribers, current values or history. §7.2 states plainly that the rich response shapes previously specified "described a service that observes traffic; that service does not exist."
- consequence-status: `GET /api/v1/status` reports what the store holds on disk. It does not report module liveness, transport health or bus time, because the store cannot know any of those.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §7.1, §7.2, §7.4, §7.5
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-SOURCES-DISAGREE] DP-CIO-23 and §7.2 describe two different registries — recorded, not resolved

- conflict: DP-CIO-23 says the namespace registry maintains "state engines, current values, and which devices publish/subscribe", enabling auto-discovery and a whole-system `composeio status`. §7.2 says the registry is a passive store that returns what was PUT plus a `name` key, computes nothing, and that a service observing traffic "does not exist."
- both-are-sources: neither is code and neither is this document's to overrule. §7.2 is the later and more specific statement and was written as an explicit correction, which is evidence but not a ruling.
- why-it-matters: the two readings support completely different tooling. A `composeio status` built on DP-CIO-23's description would render fields the server never sends — the exact failure §7.5 records for the status endpoint.
- resolution-owner: pass 2. A gap analysis against the implementation settles which description is current, and that verdict is not taken here.
- resolution-state: EMPTY
- source-section: spec/00-composeio-design-principles.smd DP-CIO-23 versus spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §7.2
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

