## 3. The REST surface, as §7 records it

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-API-CONFIG] Config — get, put, delete, checksum

- get: `GET /api/v1/config/{module_id}` returns the compiled config binary as `application/octet-stream` with **200**, or **404** `{"error": "No config for module '{module_id}'"}`.
- get-has-no-crc-header: §7.1 flags this explicitly — no `X-Config-CRC` header is set, the header previously specified does not exist, and a client wanting the checksum must call the checksum endpoint.
- put: `PUT /api/v1/config/{module_id}` with the binary as body returns **201** `{"status": "ok", "module": "{module_id}", "size": 1024}`. §7.1 notes the status is 201 and not 200, that there is no `crc` field, and that the `module` field was previously undocumented.
- put-metadata: an optional `X-ComposeIO-Metadata` header is stored verbatim as `{module_id}.meta.json`.
- put-has-no-side-effect: see [CXC-BUSSTORE-NOT-OBSERVER]. §7.1 marks this the most consequential correction in the section.
- delete: `DELETE /api/v1/config/{module_id}` removes the config and its metadata sidecar, returning **200** `{"status": "deleted", "module": "{module_id}"}`. Deleting a config that does not exist succeeds idempotently rather than 404ing.
- checksum: `GET /api/v1/config/{module_id}/checksum` — §7.1 stresses the path is `/checksum` and NOT `/crc`, which returns 404. The response is **200** with a bare lowercase hex string as `text/plain`, zero-padded to 8 digits, e.g. `a1b2c3d4` — not JSON, no `0x` prefix. **404** with the same message as `get` when absent.
- containment: `{module_id}` is a request-supplied segment and is bound by [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONTAINMENT] — it MUST resolve inside the config root. The `.bin` / `.meta.json` suffix the handler appends narrows what an escape can reach but does not prevent one; the `delete` route in particular is an arbitrary-file-REMOVAL primitive without containment, which is why the invariant is stated on the family rather than on the read routes only.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §7.1
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-API-ASSET] Assets — GET and PUT, atomic writes, and no delete route

- get: `GET /api/v1/assets/{path}` returns **200** with the asset bytes and "an appropriate Content-Type header", or **404** when not found. The path is hierarchical; the section's example is `/api/v1/assets/album_art/current.jpg`.
- content-type-derivation: §7.3 states the type is guessed from the file extension on GET, and that the request's `Content-Type` on PUT is neither required nor stored.
- content-type-table: EMPTY. The source says the type is guessed from the extension and does not enumerate which extensions are recognised or what an unrecognised one yields.
- put: `PUT /api/v1/assets/{path}` returns **201** `{"status": "ok", "path": "album_art/current.jpg", "size": 45000}`. §7.3 notes the status is 201 and not 200 and that there is **no `url` field** — a caller needing a `bus://` URL constructs it from `path`.
- put-durability: written atomically via `os.replace`, so a concurrent GET never observes a torn file; parent directories are created as needed.
- no-delete: §7.3 states `DELETE /api/v1/assets/{path}` does not exist and returns 404. There is no route to remove an asset.
- size-limits: EMPTY. No source states a maximum asset size, a request body limit, or what happens when one is exceeded.
- containment: `{path}` is a request-supplied segment and is bound by [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONTAINMENT] — it MUST resolve inside the assets root, on GET and on PUT alike. ⚠ AMENDED 2026-08-15: the sources' silence here was an IMPLEMENTATION GAP, not a documentation gap. Probe answered [CXC-BUSSTORE-PRE-CONTAINMENT]; see [CXC-BUSSTORE-EV-06].
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §7.3
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-API-ASSET-LIST] The listing route — one level, dirs first, guarded against escape

- route: `GET /api/v1/assets?prefix={prefix}`, recorded in §7.3 as previously undocumented and shipping. Omit `prefix` or leave it empty for the assets root.
- response: **200** `{"entries": [...]}` — directories first, then files, each group sorted case-insensitively by name. Each entry carries `name`, the slash-joined `path` from the assets root, and `kind` of `"dir"` or `"file"`; files also carry `size`.
- non-recursive: one level under the prefix only.
- absence-is-not-an-error: a prefix that does not exist is a valid EMPTY listing — 200 with `entries: []`, never 404.
- escape-guard: **403** when the prefix escapes the assets root (`../`). **400** on a malformed prefix, the source's example being an embedded NUL.
- dotfiles-skipped: dotfiles are omitted, which also excludes the `.{name}.tmp.{pid}.{tid}` temp files atomic writes create — so a listing never shows a torn asset.
- guard-scope: ⚠ ANSWERED 2026-08-15, and the answer was the bad one. The source stated this containment guard for the LISTING route and no equivalent rule for GET or PUT because the IMPLEMENTATION had it in exactly one place — this handler — and in none of the other six. The silence was faithful reporting of a hole. The guard is no longer specified here as a property of the listing route; it is [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONTAINMENT], a property of the SERVICE, and this route is one of its subjects. See [CXC-BUSSTORE-PRE-CONTAINMENT] and [CXC-BUSSTORE-EV-06].
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §7.3
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-API-NAMESPACE] The namespace registry — a bare array, a passive store, no delete

- list: `GET /api/v1/namespaces` returns **200** with a bare JSON array of registry entries, `[{...}, {...}]`. §7.2 flags emphatically that it is NOT wrapped in a `{"namespaces": [...]}` object.
- entry-shape: whatever was PUT, plus a `name` key set by the server. There is no fixed schema and no server-computed field.
- get: `GET /api/v1/namespaces/{namespace}` returns **200** with the entry as stored plus `name`, or **404** `{"error": "Namespace '{namespace}' not found"}`. §7.2 states that no `publishers`, `subscribers`, `current_val`, `bindings` or `history` are computed or returned unless a client stored them.
- put: `PUT /api/v1/namespaces/{namespace}`, recorded as previously undocumented and shipping — its absence "made the registry look read-only and left no specified way to populate it." Body is a JSON object; the server overwrites `name`. Returns **201** `{"status": "ok", "namespace": "{namespace}"}`.
- write-serialisation: writes are serialised under a registry lock; last write wins.
- no-delete: `DELETE /api/v1/namespaces/{namespace}` does not exist and returns 404. There is no route to remove a namespace entry.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §7.2
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-API-WORKLOAD] Workloads — definitions only, no runtime state, no delete

- list: `GET /api/v1/workloads` returns **200** with a bare JSON array of the stored definitions, NOT wrapped in `{"workloads": [...]}`.
- no-runtime-fields: §7.4 states `status`, `hub` and `uptime` are not returned — the store holds definitions and has no knowledge of what is running. Liveness is the service hub's to report.
- put: `PUT /api/v1/workloads/{id}` with a JSON definition returns **201** `{"status": "ok", "workload": "camera_frame_server"}`. §7.4 notes the status is 201 not 200 and the key is `workload` not `id`, while the server does set `id` inside the stored document.
- definition-shape: the source's worked example carries `id`, `runtime`, `entry`, `requirements`, `restart` and a `namespaces` object with `publishes` and `subscribes` lists.
- put-has-no-side-effect: §7.4 states the store writes the JSON and returns, notifying and starting nothing. A service hub must be polling or otherwise triggered; this endpoint does not reach it.
- no-delete: `DELETE /api/v1/workloads/{id}` does not exist and returns 404 — "and therefore no specified way to stop one through this API."
- get-one: EMPTY. §7.4 documents a list and a put; it states no single-workload GET route and does not say whether one exists.
- containment: `{id}` is a request-supplied segment and is bound by [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONTAINMENT] — it MUST resolve inside the workloads root.
- list-is-per-row-tolerant: one unreadable or non-JSON file in the workloads directory MUST NOT deny the whole collection — see [CXC-BUSSTORE-ERROR-BOUNDARY]. The readable definitions are served and the skipped count is reported on `X-ComposeIO-Skipped-Entries`. The header carries it because the body is a specified BARE array and adding a wrapper to report a skip would break every existing client to describe an edge case.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §7.4
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-API-STATUS] Status and health — a storage status, emphatically not a system overview

- status-route: `GET /api/v1/status` returns **200** with `service`, `version`, `uptime_seconds`, `configs`, `namespaces`, `workloads`, `storage_bytes`, `disk_free_bytes`, `data_dir` and `modules`.
- what-it-is-not: §7.5 is blunt — it reports what bus storage holds on disk, not module liveness, transport health or bus time, "because bus storage does not observe the bus and cannot know any of those." The nested health object previously specified "describes a service that does not exist; a dashboard built on it would render fields the server never sends."
- modules-field: a list of module ids that have a stored config, derived from filenames and not from liveness. §7.5 states directly that a module in this list may be powered off.
- counts-not-objects: `namespaces`, `configs` and `workloads` are counts, not objects.
- health-route: `GET /health`, recorded as previously undocumented, returns **200** with the bare body `ok` as `text/plain`. It is explicitly NOT under `/api/v1`.
- cost: EMPTY. No source states what the status route costs to serve or whether it is safe to poll.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §7.5
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-NO-DELETE] Only config has a delete route — three families are append-or-overwrite

- pattern: §7 records three separate "does not exist, returns 404" findings — for assets (§7.3), namespaces (§7.2) and workloads (§7.4). Config (§7.1) is the only family with a working DELETE, and that one is idempotent.
- consequence-stated-in-source: for workloads §7.4 draws it out — there is "no specified way to stop one through this API."
- consequence-not-stated: EMPTY. No source says whether append-only is an intended durability stance or an omission, nor what an operator should do instead. See [CXC-BUSSTORE-PRE-DELETE-STANCE].
- why-grouped: the three findings appear in three separate subsections of the source and read as three unrelated notes. Gathered into one node they are visibly a single property of the surface, which is the kind of thing conversion is for.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §7.2, §7.3, §7.4
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

