## 5. Config versioning and boot

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONFIG-CRC] CRC-32 over the whole binary

- algorithm: CRC-32 (ISO 3309, polynomial 0x04C11DB7) computed over the entire compiled config binary — the same CRC-32 used by zlib, PNG and Ethernet.
- purpose-in-source: config currency. A module compares its cached CRC against the store's to decide whether to pull.
- contrast-carried-from-source: §8.2's cross-reference notes the bus-config path uses a **CRC-16/CCITT whole-payload hash** carried in the transfer header instead, chosen so a 2 KB-class part needs no second polynomial. Two paths, two integrity primitives, both stated in the source.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §8.1, §8.2
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONFIG-LIFECYCLE] Push, pull, verify, reconfigure — with the notification step marked absent in the source itself

- flow: builder edits YAML, `composeio push` validates it against the schema, the compiler produces a compact binary, CRC-32 is computed, and the binary is uploaded with `PUT /api/v1/config/{module_id}`.
- the-gap-the-source-marks: §8.2 carries a banner reading "THE NOTIFICATION STEP DOES NOT EXIST" — the store writes the file and returns, holding no bus handle, so a `composeio push` does not reach a running module and the lifecycle completes only across a reboot unless something else polls.
- module-side: the module fetches the binary with `GET /api/v1/config/{module_id}`, verifies the CRC-32 against the header, writes binary and CRC to flash, reconfigures, and publishes discovery with the updated `config_crc`.
- live-update-path: §8.2 names [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS] as supplying the live-update path this lifecycle assumed — the head may push an unsolicited transfer when bus storage changes, making boot-time pull the degenerate case rather than a separate mechanism.
- capability-boundary-carried: the same cross-reference records that `config: http` cannot receive live updates at all, and calls that a real capability boundary rather than an implementation gap.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §8.2
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONFIG-SIZE] Under 4 KB for CH32V003, under 32 KB for ESP32 and RP2040

- constraint: compiled config binaries MUST be less than 4 KB for CH32V003 targets — a 2 KB flash budget after firmware — and less than 32 KB for ESP32 and RP2040 targets.
- enforcement-point: the `composeio push` tool validates the constraint before uploading. The store itself is not stated to enforce it.
- server-side-enforcement: EMPTY. No source says what the store does with an oversized binary, or whether it checks at all.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §8.4
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-BOOT-MODULE] The module boot sequence — cached config applied first, then a request

- first-boot: a module with empty flash publishes `config_request` with `crc=0x00000000`, waits up to 10 seconds for `config_push`, and on no response enters discovery-only mode, publishing discovery and waiting.
- warm-boot: a module with a config in flash applies the cached config IMMEDIATELY with no network dependency (DP-CIO-11), then publishes `config_request` with the cached CRC. If the store responds with new config it applies the update; if the store responds "current" it is done; if there is no response within 5 seconds it continues with the cached config.
- superseded-in-part-by-the-source-itself: §8.3 carries a cross-reference stating [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS] SUPERSEDES the retry and failure behaviour above, with two differences a builder MUST honour — retry is exponential with jitter and NEVER STOPS (base 1 s, doubling, 30 s ceiling, ±25% jitter as protocol constants), where the fixed 10 s / 5 s waits above give up; and a board with no config MUST SAY SO, on the bus if it can and on its own glass if it cannot, because "enters discovery-only mode and waits" is silent and a dark or stale panel is indistinguishable from a working one.
- jitter-rationale-carried: the jitter is load-bearing — it stops a rack of boards that lost their head together from returning in LOCKSTEP and re-creating the outage the moment it clears.
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §8.3
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-BOOT-DISPLAY] The display boot sequence — the only source describing what a display fetches

- sequence: [CXC-R-BOOT-SEQUENCE] describes a generic display device booting — initialise display hardware, connect to network, connect to the MQTT broker, announce on `_bus/discovery/{uuid}` with role and capabilities, then request config from bus_storage.
- the-fetch-as-stated: the corpus node states step 5 as `GET /api/v1/config/{uuid}`, receiving a `.compose.yaml` carrying the widget composition, namespace bindings and asset URIs.
- assets-as-stated: step 7 states the device fetches assets — fonts, icons, images — from **document_store**, caching them in PSRAM.
- config-poll: step 12 includes a config poll every 5 minutes, re-parsing and re-rendering if the config changed. Note this is a THIRD update mechanism alongside boot-time pull and the bus push path, and no source reconciles the three.
- fungibility: [CXC-R-BOOT-FUNGIBLE] states the device is generic and its behaviour entirely determined by the `.compose.yaml` it receives from bus_storage — the same hardware becoming a weather widget, an engine gauge cluster or a task dashboard by config alone.
- internal-tension: ⚠ step 5 fetches the `.compose.yaml` from the CONFIG family while [CXC-R-BOOT-FUNGIBLE] and DP-CIO-23 treat `.compose.yaml` as the config artifact and assets as a separate family; step 7 then fetches assets from a DIFFERENT SERVICE. Recorded, not resolved — see [CXC-BUSSTORE-BOOT-SOURCES-DISAGREE].
- source-section: [CXC-R-BOOT-SEQUENCE] and [CXC-R-BOOT-FUNGIBLE] in 12-composeux-render-engine.lts
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-BOOT-SOURCES-DISAGREE] Three boot descriptions, and no source reconciles them

- description-one: §8.3's module sequence — a compiled BINARY, addressed by `module_id`, fetched from the config family, verified by CRC-32, cached in flash.
- description-two: [CXC-R-BOOT-SEQUENCE]'s display sequence — a `.compose.yaml`, addressed by `uuid`, fetched from `GET /api/v1/config/{uuid}`, with assets from document_store.
- description-three: [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]'s bus path — a scene config chunked over ComposeBus, addressed by namespace, integrity by CRC-16/CCITT.
- what-is-unreconciled: whether a display's `.compose.yaml` is a "config" in the §7.1 sense or an "asset" in the §7.3 sense; whether `module_id` and `uuid` are the same identifier space; whether a display caches to flash the way §8.3's module does; and which of the three update mechanisms (boot pull, 5-minute poll, bus push) is authoritative when they disagree.
- why-recorded-rather-than-resolved: every one of these is answerable from the implementation, and answering them here would be exactly the self-certification pass 2 exists to prevent. They are stated so the analyser inherits a worklist rather than a smooth-reading document that hides four open questions.
- resolution-state: EMPTY
- source-section: spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §8.3 versus [CXC-R-BOOT-SEQUENCE] versus [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

