## 10. Ruled invariants — VERIFIED against the implementation, unlike everything above

> ⚠ **These two nodes are NOT pass-1 conversion and the document banner does not apply to them.** They
> were derived from a defect probe against a scratch instance on 2026-08-15, they state what the
> service MUST do rather than what a source said it does, and each is enforced by a test that was
> watched FAILING before the fix landed (`tests/test_bus_storage_containment.py`, 41 red -> green).
> They are marked out here rather than filed among the converted nodes because the two have opposite
> epistemic status and a reader must not have to guess which kind a node is.
>
> They are also the first CONTENT in this file, as opposed to carried prose. Everything in sections
> 1–7 still awaits `factlattice-gap-analyzer`.

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONTAINMENT] Every request-supplied segment resolves INSIDE its family root — one primitive, not one per route

- invariant: any route that turns a request-supplied path segment into a filesystem path MUST resolve that segment strictly inside the root of its family (`assets/`, `configs/`, `workloads/`). A segment resolving outside is **403**; a segment that cannot be resolved at all (an embedded NUL) is **400**. There is no route exempt from this and no such thing as a segment trusted because of where it came from.
- one-implementation: the invariant is carried by ONE primitive, `_resolve_contained(root, rel, suffix=...)`, and every handler that builds a path calls it. Stated as a structural requirement, not a coding style note: a guard that must be REMEMBERED at each of seven call sites is a guard that will be forgotten at the eighth, which is the literal history of this defect.
- three-forms-three-guards: the refusal must survive all three attack shapes, because each defeats a different naive implementation. `../` is the obvious one. `%2e%2e/` defeats any check running before `unquote` — and `_route` unquotes first, so a wire-form string check never sees it. A LEADING SLASH defeats a `..` check entirely and is a Python-specific trap: `Path("/root") / "/etc/hosts"` evaluates to `/etc/hosts`, silently DISCARDING the base, with no `..` anywhere in the request.
- resolve-do-not-grep: containment is decided by resolving the path and testing ancestry, never by string-matching for `..`. A string check both over-rejects a legitimate name like `fw/v1..2/image.bin` and under-rejects the absolute form. Resolution also catches a symlink out of the root, which no string check can see.
- positive-control-is-part-of-the-invariant: the guard MUST NOT reject legitimate nested paths. `bus://assets/album_art/current.jpg` resolution and the fleet's boot-time config fetch run through these routes; a containment guard that also breaks them has traded a vulnerability for a fleet-wide outage. Any test of this invariant carries the paired positive control or it is not a test of this invariant.
- why-it-was-missed: the guard was authored 2026-07-30 (`30255a0`) as a local detail INSIDE `_handle_asset_list`, born with a new feature and scoped to it. It was never lifted to the seam, so it was structurally unavailable to the six handlers that already existed — and equally unavailable to every handler added since. This is the failure mode [DX-NARROW-WAIST] names: the correct behaviour existed, in one place, reachable by nobody. Six sibling routes did not "forget" the guard; they were never able to reach it.
- containment-is-not-authentication: this invariant bounds WHICH paths a caller may address. It says nothing about WHO may call, and every route remains openly callable — see [CXC-BUSSTORE-PRE-AUTH], which this does not answer.
- enforced-by: `tests/test_bus_storage_containment.py`, parametrized over routes × attack forms. A new route is added to `_ESCAPE_ROUTES` and is covered; a new route that is NOT added is the one gap this design still has, and it is a visible one-line omission rather than an invisible absent guard.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

### [CXC-BUSSTORE-ERROR-BOUNDARY] Every request gets an HTTP status line, and one bad row never denies a collection

- invariant-response: no request may terminate without an HTTP response. A malformed JSON body is **400**; an unhandled server-side failure is **500** with a logged traceback. A closed connection with no status line is a defect, not an error report.
- why-it-costs-more-than-it-looks: a `RemoteDisconnected` with no status line tells an operator nothing about which layer failed, so it sends them to the network — sockets, firewall, MTU, the bus master's health — to debug what is a 400 from a JSON parser. The service being UP the whole time is what makes it expensive: every signal says healthy. Hours, and only the operator pays them.
- invariant-collection: a collection route MUST NOT let one unreadable member deny the whole collection. `GET /api/v1/workloads` serves every definition it can parse and skips those it cannot. One corrupt file is a corrupt file, not an outage of every workload in the store.
- skips-must-be-visible: a survived skip that is silent is worse than the crash it replaced — the caller cannot distinguish a partial listing from a complete one, and acts on the partial. The count rides on `X-ComposeIO-Skipped-Entries` and the skip is logged at WARNING. The header rather than the body because the bare-array shape is specified ([CXC-BUSSTORE-API-WORKLOAD]) and breaking every client to describe an edge case is the wrong trade.
- boundary-must-not-double-respond: the handler tracks whether a response has already begun, so a failure AFTER the first byte logs rather than writing a second status line into the same stream. A boundary that corrupts the response it was added to protect is a downgrade.
- boundary-must-not-swallow: an unknown route is still **404** and `/health` is still **200**. An error boundary that turns every outcome into a 500 has replaced one uninformative answer with another.
- enforced-by: `tests/test_bus_storage_containment.py::TestRequestErrorBoundary`, including the negative controls for the two preceding properties.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUSSTORE]

